the world has been paying tribute to  queen elizabeth ii in her role as head  of the commonwealth of nations the queen  helped advance a new international order  of peace and prosperity  she ascended the throne only a few years  after the petition of india both  pakistan and bangladesh were created out  of india all three members of the  commonwealth but not without their  differences  prime minister narendra modi met queen  elizabeth twice during trips to the uk  he warmly remembered the queen showing  him a handkerchief given to her by  mahatma gandhi the pm tweeted she'll be  remembered as a stalwart of our times  she provided inspiring leadership to her  nation and people personified dignity  and decency in public life  our delhi bureau chief amrita chima  joins us so we saw modi's message of  condolence what's been the reaction in  india  ben tributes have been pouring in since  the news came in and the news came in in  india late last night so the most of the  newspapers didn't catch more than just a  short headline on the news of the  queen's passing but there's a lot of  stuff online and the televisions are  giving a lot of coverage a lot of the  comments are coming from politicians one  local politician described uh  queen elizabeth as a person who had  shown courage wisdom and praised her  service uh to the nation and then you  had another person talking about you  know she's a wonderful person she was a  true friend of india so a lot of  coverage on the queen's passing here in  delhi  the queen ascended the throne just after  indian independence she really was the  face of the evolving relationship  between india and the uk wasn't she  in many ways yes ben because she became  a queen five years after india got  independence in 1947 and she came to  india 11 years later now her first visit  was in 1961 and she talked about the  warmth and hospitality of indians and  she also talked about the richness and  diversity of india which she said was an  inspiration to people like her so her  next visit then was uh in  1983 and that's the time when the  commonwealth heads of government were  meeting here in delhi and that was also  the time that she famously gave mother  teresa an order of merit and at that  time indira gandhi india's female prime  minister was there and it was wonderful  to see these pictures we've been seeing  in recent hours of the two strong  competent and elegant ladies obviously  enjoying each other's company and the  final visit went to india was in 1997  and that was when india was marking 50  years of independence and that's when  the queen for the first time  acknowledged the colonial history  between the two countries and she talked  about difficult episodes of colonial  history and mentioned specifically a  famous massacre at julia wallabang so  obviously she had acknowledged something  that meant a lot to people here in india  and rita do you have any personal  memories of the queen  well actually i have to you know when i  was a very little girl i was obviously  quite kind of mesmerized by the whole  royalty thing so i wrote a letter to the  queen uh asking about her and asking her  for a photo obviously i didn't get a  reply back from her but i did get a  reply from her lady in waiting saying  she'd been instructed by her majesty to  thank me for my letter  and the second episode was actually uh  much later when i was a student  yeah and that's it and the second time  ben was when i was a student in england  at oxford university and she was our  chief guest at a rhodes scholars  function so i remember that she came by  and you know we were all dazzled by a  very neon colored outfit she just walked  past me but prince philip he stopped by  and exchanged a few words uh with me but  that wasn't good enough for me i wanted  to talk to the queen  and reader uh tell me what is the  commonwealth now without queen elizabeth  ii  i think most people will say quite  diminished because many people felt in  the comment well that she was the glue  that held this grouping together because  people had personal regard for her even  though some people feel that this whole  commonwealth thing is a bit outdated but  they felt that till she is there that  this organization was worth being a  member of now but things are changing  also in india as we saw just very  recently at the indian prime minister he  renamed this street just behind me it  was called king's way earlier linked  with the colonial past it's now been  named the path of duty and what he said  the prime minister was india has got rid  of a symbol of slavery so you see that  there's a great desire here in this  government to get rid of the colonial  legacy and i'm sure that will also have  an impact on india's links with the with  with the commonwealth and reeduca for us  in delhi thank you very much  reactions to the queen's passing have  been pouring in from around the region  singapore's leader lee shen long  described elizabeth ii as the very heart  and soul of the uk taiwanese president  tsai inguin said her country remembers  and celebrates her life of leadership  and service which set an example for  people around the  world and japan's prime minister fumio  kishida also paid tribute to the queen  for strengthening ties between his  country and the uk  this is a huge loss  not only for the british people  japan will be with the people of britain  in overcoming this deep sorrow  china has also offered condolences but  the queen's passing is more acutely felt  in hong kong the british ruled the  territory for over 150 years before  handing it back to the chinese in 1997.  people have been gathering outside the  british consulate the union jack flying  at half-mast  elizabeth visited hong kong twice during  her reign in 1975 and 86 when it was  still a british outpost here are some  reactions from hong kongers  i miss the queen very much and i'm very  grateful to her for the many years she  ruled over hong kong  when i learned of her passing away this  morning i felt a pang of sadness  it's possible that we've always had a  firm handle on the circumstances of the  british royal family as i grew up this  feels both distant and close  i remember her visiting hong kong in the  early days of her reign  grandiose and influential oh the general  public felt she was beautiful and  wonderful  and over to dw correspondent phoebe kong  tell us more about the reaction in hong  kong  oh well queen elizabeth and the royal  family is of course no stranger to hong  kong residents and  like just last night when the news broke  many people took to social media to  mourn the death of the queen and today  more and more people online offline on  paying tribute to this legendary [ __ ]  that many people went to the british  consulate general in hong kong to  leave flowers and also messages  to the book of all  condolences and  people saying that they pay a respect  and also gratitude to the queen that  have um like rude hong kong uh for a  decade before hong kong was handed back  to china and uh also there was some like  reaction from the government that  despite at the times of some kind of the  tensions between china and hong kong  government and also the uk  but then hong kong chief executive john  lee gave a short statement saying that  she  he expressed profound sadness and  condolences  to the death of the queen and he  described that the queen was greatly  respected and praised by the british  people so that's the reaction from the  hong kong government and when we look at  the media coverage that um like of  course this is one of like the biggest  news today in hong kong because people  feel so connected with um the uk and  also the queen herself but then um  because of the political climate here  and and also kind of like a sensitive uh  matters of covering about the uk  royal family that some of the  media appear to have some  self-censorship that i've heard from  some of the friends from um the newsroom  working in hong kong they said like some  editors like have the cell center some  of the news coverage to tune down on the  coverage and not to like play it too big  can you describe for me in more detail  that connection there  to the uk and the monarchy especially  for the older generation  uh yes hong kong had been like um like  under british food for over 150 years  and um and the most important era that  is the post-war era was under the reign  of the queen elizabeth and uh  interestingly like people in hong kong  call the queen as um the the boss lady  that is the nickname given um to the  queen by the general public that is like  a cantonese nickname so so so we can  feel the sense of like special  attachment and special connection  between the hong kong um people and then  and to the queen herself and then like  but that's not only limited to the older  generation is also um the younger  generation that feels that like um the  the greatest legacy left by the queen  and the colonial governments that  she led is that um like making hong kong  from a fish village to one of the most  successful international cities by like  laying like important infrastructure to  at the society um say the judiciary  educational the medical system and and  the very important like administrative  uh system that that have left hong kong  so successful in many aspects so um for  many people um the queen represents uh  the those good old times of hong kong  that especially when hong kong is facing  like political upheavals after the  hanover so many people like miss those  times like under the colonial rule and  like even until today we we still see  some like um science and footprints of  of the queen like many  important facilities like the hospitals  some public schools are named after  queen elizabeth and and even some coins  like with her face on it still circulate  until today so she's kind of like still  living memories for for many residents  in hong kong  phoebe kong on the boss lady and her  footprint in hong kong thank you  well apart from the uk queen elizabeth  was the monarch of 14 countries one of  those was papua new guinea which like  nearby australia and new zealand woke up  today with a new head of state king  charles iii here's png's prime minister  james marapi  from the mountains  the valleys  the planes the islands and the lowlands  of our country  in the 600 plus islands that we call  papua new guinea  the entire nation stand  in  silence  and offer our  deepest analysis  well that's it for this week more  coverage on the queen's legacy on our  website we leave you with her many trips  to asia during her long reign bye-bye  [Music]  [Music]  you

